.TH MGREP 1 "August 2026" "mgrep" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mgrep \- fast, multithreaded literal and regex search
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mgrep
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " PATTERN " [ PATH ...]
.br
.B mgrep
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " PATTERN " \-
.br
.B mgrep
.RI [ "FILTER OPTIONS" ] " --files " [ PATH ...]
.br
.B mgrep
.RI [ "FILTER OPTIONS" ] " --file NAME " [ PATH ...]
.br
.B mgrep
.RI [ --no-color ] " --themes"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mgrep
searches files or standard input for a literal or regular expression pattern.
It is designed around fast file discovery, automatic literal fast paths, and
batched output.
.PP
Without source or line-number output options, normal file searches print the path
of each file containing a match. Options such as
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-o ,
and
.B \-O
select more detailed output.
.PP
If
.I PATH
is
.BR \- ,
standard input is searched at that point in the operand sequence. If no path is
given and standard input is redirected, standard input is searched automatically.
Multiple paths, file lists, and standard-input operands may be combined.
.SH PATTERN
Patterns use RE2's Perl-like regular expression syntax by default. Plain patterns
are detected automatically and retain the fast literal matcher. Use
.B --literal
to treat every pattern character literally.
.PP
Matching is byte-oriented and regex matching initially uses Latin-1 semantics. The
.B --ignore-case
option enables case-insensitive matching.
.PP
Regex works with count, quiet, only-matching, source, line-number, context, and
path-only output. One-line and multiline regex searches currently report an error
instead of silently changing their behavior.
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSION REFERENCE
The following is a quick reference for the RE2 syntax accepted by mgrep. Shells
also interpret characters such as
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.BR $ ,
backslash, and parentheses, so patterns should normally be enclosed in single
quotes.
.SS Ordinary characters and quoting
Most characters match themselves. These characters have special meaning in a
regular expression:
.PP
.RS
.B "\e . ^ $ | ( ) [ ] { } * + ?"
.RE
.PP
Precede a metacharacter with a backslash to match it literally, for example
.B \e.
to match a period or
.B \e\e
to match a backslash.
.B \eQ...\eE
quotes all text between
.B \eQ
and
.BR \eE .
The
.B --literal
option instead treats the entire pattern as literal text.
.SS Concatenation and alternatives
.TP
.B xy
Match
.I x
followed by
.IR y .
.TP
.B x|y
Match either
.I x
or
.IR y .
Alternatives are tried from left to right. Concatenation binds more tightly than
alternation, so
.B 'cat|dog'
matches either word.
.SS Repetition
The repetition operators apply to the expression immediately before them.
.TP
.B x*
Match zero or more
.IR x .
.TP
.B x+
Match one or more
.IR x .
.TP
.B x?
Match zero or one
.IR x .
.TP
.BI x{ n }
Match exactly
.I n
copies of
.IR x .
.TP
.BI x{ n ,}
Match at least
.I n
copies.
.TP
.BI x{ n , m }
Match from
.I n
through
.I m
copies. RE2 rejects repetition counts greater than 1000.
.PP
Repetitions are greedy by default: they prefer the longest match. Append
.B ?
to prefer the shortest match:
.BR *? ,
.BR +? ,
.BR ?? ,
.BR {n}? ,
.BR {n,}? ,
and
.BR {n,m}? .
The
.B U
flag reverses greediness. Possessive repetitions such as
.B *+
are not supported.
.SS Groups and flags
.TP
.B (re)
Numbered capturing group. Groups are numbered by opening parenthesis, beginning
with 1; group 0 means the entire match.
.TP
.B (?:re)
Non-capturing group.
.TP
.B (?P<name>re)
Named capturing group. The alternate spelling
.B (?<name>re)
is not accepted by the RE2 version used by mgrep.
.TP
.B (?imsU)
Set flags for the remainder of the current group.
.TP
.B (?imsU:re)
Set flags only while matching
.IR re .
Place
.B -
before flags to clear them, as in
.B (?i-s:re)
or
.BR (?-i) .
.PP
The flags are
.B i
for case-insensitive matching,
.B m
for multi-line anchors,
.B s
to let dot match newline, and
.B U
to make repetition ungreedy. A pattern that can match a newline is currently
rejected by mgrep even though RE2 recognizes the syntax.
.SS Any character and character classes
.TP
.B .
Match any character except newline. With flag
.BR s ,
dot also matches newline.
.TP
.B [xyz]
Match one listed character.
.TP
.B [^xyz]
Match one character not listed.
.TP
.B [a-z]
Match one character in the inclusive range from
.B a
through
.BR z .
.PP
Items can be combined inside a class, as in
.BR [A-Za-z0-9_] .
Inside a class,
.B ^
is special only at the beginning,
.B -
denotes a range, and
.B ]
ends the class; escape these characters when they are meant literally. Character
class intersection and subtraction are not supported.
.SS Perl character classes
.TP
.B \ed
ASCII digit:
.BR [0-9] .
.TP
.B \eD
Any character other than an ASCII digit.
.TP
.B \es
ASCII whitespace:
.BR [\et\en\ef\er\ ] .
.TP
.B \eS
Any character other than ASCII whitespace.
.TP
.B \ew
ASCII word character:
.BR [0-9A-Za-z_] .
.TP
.B \eW
Any character other than an ASCII word character.
.PP
These escapes can also be used inside bracketed character classes.
.SS Unicode character classes
.TP
.BI \ep{ name }
Match a character in the named Unicode general category or script, for example
.BR \epL ,
.BR \ep{Greek} ,
or
.BR \ep{Han} .
.TP
.BI \eP{ name }
Match a character not in the named Unicode category or script.
.PP
The braces may be omitted for a one-letter Unicode category, as in
.BR \epL .
These escapes are also accepted inside bracketed character classes. Because
mgrep configures RE2 for Latin-1 byte-oriented matching, only characters
representable in that mode can match; the ASCII classes are usually clearer for
byte searches.
.SS ASCII character classes
The following named classes can be used inside brackets. Their negated forms use
.B ^
after the first colon, for example
.BR [[:^digit:]] .
.TP
.B [[:alnum:]]
.BR [0-9A-Za-z]
.TP
.B [[:alpha:]]
.BR [A-Za-z]
.TP
.B [[:ascii:]]
.BR [\ex00-\ex7F]
.TP
.B [[:blank:]]
Space or tab.
.TP
.B [[:cntrl:]]
.BR [\ex00-\ex1F\ex7F]
.TP
.B [[:digit:]]
.BR [0-9]
.TP
.B [[:graph:]]
Visible characters from
.B !
through
.BR ~ .
.TP
.B [[:lower:]]
.BR [a-z]
.TP
.B [[:print:]]
ASCII space through
.BR ~ .
.TP
.B [[:punct:]]
ASCII punctuation characters.
.TP
.B [[:space:]]
.BR [\et\en\ev\ef\er\ ]
.TP
.B [[:upper:]]
.BR [A-Z]
.TP
.B [[:word:]]
.BR [0-9A-Za-z_]
.TP
.B [[:xdigit:]]
.BR [0-9A-Fa-f]
.SS Anchors and empty matches
.TP
.B ^
Match at the beginning of text, or the beginning of a line with flag
.BR m .
.TP
.B $
Match at the end of text, like
.BR \ez ,
or before a newline at the end of text with flag
.BR m .
.TP
.B \eA
Match only at the beginning of text.
.TP
.B \ez
Match only at the end of text.
.TP
.B \eb
Match an ASCII word boundary: one side is an ASCII
.B \ew
character and the other is not.
.TP
.B \eB
Match where there is no ASCII word boundary.
.PP
.BR \eZ ,
.BR \eG ,
and
.B \eK
are not supported.
.SS Escape sequences
.TP
.B \ea
Bell, byte 0x07.
.TP
.B \ef
Form feed, byte 0x0C.
.TP
.B \et
Horizontal tab, byte 0x09.
.TP
.B \en
Newline, byte 0x0A. Patterns containing newline escapes are currently rejected
by mgrep.
.TP
.B \er
Carriage return, byte 0x0D.
.TP
.B \ev
Vertical tab, byte 0x0B.
.TP
.B \e123
The byte specified by up to three octal digits.
.TP
.B \ex7F
The byte specified by two hexadecimal digits.
.TP
.B \ex{7F}
The character specified by hexadecimal digits. In mgrep's Latin-1 regex mode,
the value must fit in one byte, from 00 through FF.
.TP
.B \eC
Match one byte, including a byte that is part of an encoded character.
.PP
Escaping a punctuation character quotes that character. C escapes such as
.B \eb
for backspace are not generally accepted when the same spelling is a regex
operator; use an octal or hexadecimal escape when necessary.
.SS Unsupported Perl-style constructs
RE2 guarantees linear-time matching by omitting constructs that require
backtracking or access to previously matched text. Consequently mgrep does not
support backreferences such as
.B \e1
or
.BR \ek<name> ;
lookahead or lookbehind such as
.BR (?=re) ,
.BR (?!re) ,
.BR (?<=re) ,
or
.BR (?<!re) ;
atomic groups
.BR (?>re) ;
conditionals;
recursion;
subroutine calls;
branch-reset groups;
or embedded code. Perl extended-mode comments and whitespace with
.B (?x)
are also unsupported.
.SH SEARCH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " --help
Display concise command help and exit successfully.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " --invert-match
Select lines that do not contain the pattern. This cannot be combined with
.BR --only-matching .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " --ignore-case
Match case-insensitively.
.TP
.B \-r
Recursively search below directory operands. Without this option, directory
handling is limited to the requested directory level.
.TP
.BI \-m " NUM"
Read at most
.I NUM
lines from each input. A value of zero disables the limit.
.TP
.BI --max-depth " NUM"
Descend at most
.I NUM
path levels below each explicit directory operand and enable recursive search.
The directory operand itself is depth zero, files directly inside it are depth
one, and a value of zero prevents directory descent while still permitting
explicit file operands.
.TP
.B --literal
Treat every pattern character literally.
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " --count
Print the number of selected lines for each input that has at least one selected
line. Count output takes precedence over normal, one-line, and only-matching output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " --quiet
Print nothing and stop as soon as the requested search status is known. Exit status
still indicates whether a match was found. Quiet mode takes precedence over other
output modes.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " --one-line
Print each selected source line as one physical output line. File records contain
the path, an optional line number, and a match-centered source excerpt. Output is
capped at 100 visible ASCII columns; ANSI control sequences do not count toward
the limit. Long paths and source excerpts are marked with an ellipsis.
.IP
One-line mode cannot be combined with
.BR \-B ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR --heading ,
.BR --only-matching ,
or a multiline pattern.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " --only-matching
Print only the matching text, once per non-overlapping occurrence. File output
includes its path; add
.B \-l
to include line numbers. This cannot be combined with
.BR --invert-match .
.TP
.B \-l
Include the matching line number in output.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the source line containing each selected match.
.TP
.BI \-B " NUM"
Print up to
.I NUM
lines before each selected line.
.TP
.BI \-A " NUM"
Print up to
.I NUM
lines after each selected line.
.TP
.B \-n
Print an additional newline after each result.
.TP
.B --heading
Force line and source output below one heading for each matching file. This layout
is enabled automatically when standard output is a terminal and source, line-number,
or context output is requested.
.TP
.B --verbose
Print completion and match statistics after searching. Quiet mode suppresses this
summary.
.TP
.B --null
Terminate path-only output records with a NUL byte instead of a newline. This also
applies to
.B --files
output and makes paths containing newlines unambiguous. It cannot be combined with
line-oriented or verbose output, or with a standard-input search. Use
.B --no-color
when passing the resulting paths to another program.
.SH INPUT AND FILTER OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Search all files. This permits binary-looking input and disables the normal ignore
rules and extension-based skips. It may substantially increase work.
.TP
.B --files
List the files mgrep would search instead of searching their contents. No pattern
is required. With no path operand, this mode recursively lists from the current
directory.
.TP
.BI --file " NAME"
Recursively find files whose basename exactly equals
.IR NAME .
No search pattern is required, wildcard characters are treated literally, and the
option may be repeated to select multiple names. With no path operand, search from
the current directory. Exact-name selectors combine with type, extension, glob,
depth, exclusion, and NUL-output options.
.TP
.BR --ff " " FILE ", " --files-from " " FILE
Read newline-delimited input paths from
.IR FILE .
Use
.B \-
as the file-list name to read the path list from standard input.
.TP
.BR --files-from0 " " FILE ", " --null-files-from " " FILE
Read NUL-delimited input paths from
.IR FILE .
The NUL form supports paths containing newlines.
.TP
.BI --type " TYPE"
Search only files belonging to a named type. Supported names are
.BR header ,
.BR headers ,
.BR source ,
.BR sources ,
.BR cpp ,
and
.BR c++ .
.TP
.BI --ext " EXT[,EXT...]"
Search only files whose extensions occur in the comma-separated list. A leading
dot is optional.
.TP
.BI --glob " GLOB"
Search only paths matching
.IR GLOB .
Use this when a name or path is only partially known; use
.B --file
for a known exact basename.
The matcher supports
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
and recursive
.B **
components.
.TP
.BI --exclude-glob " GLOB"
Skip paths matching
.IR GLOB .
May be specified more than once.
.TP
.B --
End option parsing. This permits patterns or paths beginning with a hyphen.
.SH APPEARANCE OPTIONS
Built-in themes render non-matching source text in subdued ANSI color 248 so
highlighted matches remain prominent.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " --pretty
Compatibility alias. ANSI colors are enabled by default.
.TP
.BR \-t " THEME" ", " --theme " THEME"
Select a color theme. Supported themes are
.BR blue ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR purple ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR orange ,
.BR pink ,
.BR mono ,
.BR bright ,
.BR gruvbox ,
.BR nord ,
.BR dracula ,
and
.BR nebula .
.TP
.B --themes
Print a compact fake search result in every built-in theme and exit successfully.
No pattern or path is required. Use
.B --no-color
to print the previews without ANSI control sequences.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI color output, including concise help when specified before
.BR \-h .
.TP
.BI --colors " COMPONENT:ATTR:VALUE"
Override one color attribute. Components are
.BR path ,
.BR file ,
.BR line ,
.BR match ,
and
.BR source .
Attributes are
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ,
and
.BR style .
Foreground and background values may be a number from 0 through 255 or one of
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR purple ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
.BR gray ,
or
.BR grey .
Styles are
.BR bold ,
.BR dim ,
.BR italic ,
.BR underline ,
and
.BR reverse .
The option may be repeated. For example:
.PP
.RS
.B --colors match:fg:magenta --colors source:fg:cyan
.RE
.SH DEFAULT FILE SELECTION
Unless
.B \-a
is used, mgrep skips binary-looking input, hidden directories, common build and
cache directories, archives, media, object files, and files without extensions.
It also loads ignore rules from
.IR $HOME/.ignore
when that file exists. Explicit file operands remain subject to active type,
extension, and glob filters.
.SH HOME IGNORE RULES
During normal searches, mgrep reads
.IR $HOME/.ignore .
Leading and trailing whitespace is removed. Empty lines, comments beginning with
.BR # ,
and negation rules beginning with
.B !
are ignored. Negation is not currently supported.
.PP
A plain name skips files and directories with that basename. A trailing slash
makes a rule directory-only. A leading
.B *
creates a basename suffix rule, such as
.B *.generated
or
.BR *cache/ .
Directory prefixes are discarded, and rules containing
.B ?
or
.B [
are ignored. This is intentionally a limited subset rather than full gitignore
syntax.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Locates the optional
.I .ignore
file used by normal searches.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one match was found, concise help was displayed, or
.B --files
completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The search completed successfully but found no matches.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred, such as invalid options, an invalid option combination, a
missing pattern, an invalid path, or an unreadable file list.
.PP
With
.BR --quiet ,
a found match may take precedence over errors encountered later because the search
stops as soon as success is known.
.SH EXAMPLES
Recursively find files containing a fixed string:
.PP
.RS
.B "mgrep -r 'int main(' ."
.RE
.PP
Print matching source with line numbers:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep -rls needle src
.RE
.PP
Print compact one-line records:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep -rol needle src
.RE
.PP
Print each matched occurrence:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep -rO needle src
.RE
.PP
Search redirected standard input without colors:
.PP
.RS
.B command | mgrep --no-color needle
.RE
.PP
List C and C++ source files mgrep would search:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep --files --type source .
.RE
.PP
Find every file named
.I main.cpp
below the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep --file main.cpp .
.RE
.PP
Safely pass matching paths to another command:
.PP
.RS
.B "mgrep --no-color --null -r needle . | xargs -0 command"
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.ignore
Optional user ignore rules loaded during normal file searches.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR rg (1)
